1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,051
This is ECE 358, video 20a. The topic is hierarchical routing, OSPF areas, and the basics of BGP.

2
00:00:11,501 --> 00:00:16,813
After this video, you can explain why the Internet splits routing into two levels.

3
00:00:17,263 --> 00:00:21,785
You can describe hierarchical OSPF and its router types.

4
00:00:22,235 --> 00:00:30,982
You can explain how BGP advertises paths, with eBGP, iBGP, AS-PATH and NEXT-HOP.

5
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:43,059
And you can show how BGP and OSPF together fill a forwarding table. Video 20b continues with route selection, policy, and the question bank.

6
00:00:43,509 --> 00:00:52,447
Up to now, our routing study was ideal. All routers were identical, and the network was flat. This is not true in practice.

7
00:00:52,897 --> 00:00:57,633
The first problem is scale. The Internet has billions of destinations.

8
00:00:58,083 --> 00:01:02,137
No router can store all of these destinations in its table.

9
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:09,733
Also, if all routers exchange their routing tables, this traffic uses up the capacity of the links.

10
00:01:10,183 --> 00:01:16,071
The second problem is administrative autonomy. The Internet is a network of networks.

11
00:01:16,521 --> 00:01:20,489
Each network admin wants to control routing in its own network.

12
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:34,166
The solution is to group the routers into autonomous systems, or ASes. Each AS has one admin. The picture shows three: AS1, AS2 and AS3.

13
00:01:34,616 --> 00:01:40,717
So routing now has two levels. Routing inside one AS is intra-AS routing.

14
00:01:41,167 --> 00:01:45,242
Routing between ASes is inter-AS routing.

15
00:01:45,692 --> 00:01:54,865
Here are three autonomous systems: AS1, AS2 and AS3. Each AS is a group of routers under one admin.

16
00:01:55,315 --> 00:02:02,953
Thick lines connect gateway routers of different ASes: 1c to 2a, and 2c to 3a.

17
00:02:03,403 --> 00:02:11,893
Inside each AS, an intra-AS routing algorithm runs. The admin of each AS selects this algorithm.

18
00:02:12,343 --> 00:02:20,898
Between the ASes, an inter-AS routing algorithm runs. It goes over the links between the gateway routers.

19
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:26,639
Each router has one forwarding table. Here is the table of router 1d.

20
00:02:27,089 --> 00:02:32,507
Intra-AS routing sets the entries for destinations inside AS1.

21
00:02:32,957 --> 00:02:40,765
For destinations outside AS1, inter-AS routing and intra-AS routing set the entries together.

22
00:02:41,215 --> 00:02:46,570
Here AS1 has two gateways. Gateway 1b connects to AS3.

23
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,495
Gateway 1c connects to AS2.

24
00:02:49,945 --> 00:02:59,523
Router 1d gets a datagram for destination Z, outside AS1. 1d must send it to one of the two gateway routers.

25
00:02:59,973 --> 00:03:11,835
So AS1 inter-domain routing has two jobs. Job 1: learn which destinations are reachable through AS2, and which are reachable through AS3.

26
00:03:12,285 --> 00:03:16,829
Here, gateway 1c learns that Y is reachable through AS2.

27
00:03:17,279 --> 00:03:22,121
And gateway 1b learns that X and Z are reachable through AS3.

28
00:03:22,571 --> 00:03:32,683
Job 2: propagate this reachability information to all routers in AS1. The purple arrows show the gateways telling the other routers.

29
00:03:33,133 --> 00:03:39,683
Now 1d knows that Z goes through gateway 1b. It forwards the datagram toward 1b.

30
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:43,589
In the Internet, BGP does these two jobs.

31
00:03:44,039 --> 00:03:53,276
Inside an AS, three intra-AS routing protocols are common. The first is RIP, the Routing Information Protocol.

32
00:03:53,726 --> 00:03:59,934
RIP is classic distance vector. Neighbours exchange distance vectors every 30 seconds.

33
00:04:00,384 --> 00:04:02,709
RIP is no longer widely used.

34
00:04:03,159 --> 00:04:08,301
The second is EIGRP. It is also based on distance vectors.

35
00:04:08,751 --> 00:04:19,993
EIGRP was Cisco-proprietary for decades. It became open in 2013, as RFC 7868.

36
00:04:20,443 --> 00:04:27,782
The third is OSPF, Open Shortest Path First. It is widely used, and it is link-state routing.

37
00:04:28,232 --> 00:04:32,712
The IS-IS protocol is about the same as OSPF.

38
00:04:33,162 --> 00:04:37,706
From here on, we use OSPF as the intra-AS protocol.

39
00:04:38,156 --> 00:04:48,993
OSPF means Open Shortest Path First. Open means that the protocol is publicly available. The picture shows the routers of one AS.

40
00:04:49,443 --> 00:05:00,345
OSPF is classic link-state routing. Each router floods OSPF link-state advertisements to all other routers in the entire AS.

41
00:05:00,795 --> 00:05:08,731
OSPF sends these advertisements directly over IP. It does not use TCP or UDP.

42
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:15,495
The link costs can use different metrics, for example bandwidth or delay. The admin sets them.

43
00:05:15,945 --> 00:05:19,529
So each router has the full topology of the AS.

44
00:05:19,979 --> 00:05:25,889
Each router runs Dijkstra's algorithm on this topology and computes its forwarding table.

45
00:05:26,339 --> 00:05:33,464
For security, all OSPF messages are authenticated. This prevents malicious intrusion.

46
00:05:33,914 --> 00:05:47,674
A large AS has many routers. Flooding link-state advertisements to all of them costs much traffic. Hierarchical OSPF reduces this overhead with a two-level hierarchy.

47
00:05:48,124 --> 00:05:54,439
Level 1 is the local areas. Here there are three: area 1, area 2 and area 3.

48
00:05:54,889 --> 00:05:58,153
Level 2 is one backbone, called area 0.

49
00:05:58,603 --> 00:06:09,291
Link-state advertisements are flooded only inside one area, or only inside the backbone. The purple arrows show a flood that stays in area 1.

50
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:17,911
So each router knows the detailed topology of its own area. For other destinations, it only knows the direction.

51
00:06:18,361 --> 00:06:26,383
There are four types of routers. Local routers, also called internal routers, flood link state in their area only.

52
00:06:26,833 --> 00:06:35,537
Local routers compute routes inside the area. For outside destinations, they forward packets to the area border router.

53
00:06:35,987 --> 00:06:40,083
Area border routers belong to an area and to the backbone.

54
00:06:40,533 --> 00:06:49,429
Area border routers summarize the distances to destinations in their own area. They advertise these summaries in the backbone.

55
00:06:49,879 --> 00:06:53,740
Backbone routers run OSPF limited to the backbone.

56
00:06:54,190 --> 00:06:58,350
Boundary routers connect the AS to other ASes.

57
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:05,541
One more rule from the slide: both sides of a link always belong to the same OSPF area.

58
00:07:05,991 --> 00:07:16,914
BGP, the Border Gateway Protocol, is the de facto inter-domain routing protocol. The slides call it the glue that holds the Internet together.

59
00:07:17,364 --> 00:07:26,153
BGP lets a border router advertise its existence, and the destinations it can reach, to the rest of the Internet.

60
00:07:26,603 --> 00:07:33,793
Here is the slide example. The University of Waterloo network is AS 12093.

61
00:07:34,243 --> 00:07:41,944
Its main IPv4 block is 129 dot 97 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16.

62
00:07:42,394 --> 00:07:47,685
It connects to ORION, AS 26677.

63
00:07:48,135 --> 00:08:04,220
A BGP router in UW sends this advertisement to a BGP router of ORION: AS 12093, and the block 129 dot 97 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16.

64
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:18,259
BGP has two parts. eBGP is external BGP. With eBGP, BGP routers get subnet reachability information from neighbouring ASes.

65
00:08:18,709 --> 00:08:28,331
This information is a sequence of ASes and the networks at the end. Here, it is AS 12093 and its block.

66
00:08:28,781 --> 00:08:46,103
iBGP is internal BGP. With iBGP, BGP routers propagate this reachability information to the other routers in the same AS. So iBGP carries external routes inside the AS.

67
00:08:46,553 --> 00:08:56,729
Together, eBGP and iBGP find good routes to other networks. They use two inputs: reachability information, and policy.

68
00:08:57,179 --> 00:09:02,790
Here are the three ASes again. The solid lines are physical links.

69
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:10,280
eBGP sessions connect routers in different ASes. The red dashed lines show them.

70
00:09:10,730 --> 00:09:12,906
Here, 1c talks to 2a.

71
00:09:13,356 --> 00:09:15,404
And 2c talks to 3a.

72
00:09:15,854 --> 00:09:25,027
iBGP sessions connect the routers inside one AS. They are logical connections, shown as blue dotted lines.

73
00:09:25,477 --> 00:09:32,923
Each pair of routers in an AS has one iBGP session. For example, 1a and 1c.

74
00:09:33,373 --> 00:09:44,637
An iBGP session can go over several physical links. From 1a to 1c, the data goes over the links 1a to 1b and 1b to 1c.

75
00:09:45,087 --> 00:09:54,772
The gateway routers run both protocols. They run eBGP to the neighbour AS, and iBGP inside their own AS.

76
00:09:55,222 --> 00:09:59,531
Here the gateway routers are 1c, 2a, 2c and 3a.

77
00:09:59,981 --> 00:10:10,008
In a BGP session, two BGP routers, called peers, exchange BGP messages. Here 2c and 3a are peers.

78
00:10:10,458 --> 00:10:13,978
The peers use a semi-permanent TCP connection.

79
00:10:14,428 --> 00:10:25,393
With these messages, a router advertises paths to different destination network prefixes that it can reach. So BGP is a path vector protocol.

80
00:10:25,843 --> 00:10:36,659
Example: AS3 gateway 3a advertises the path AS3, X to AS2 gateway 2c. X is a destination network prefix.

81
00:10:37,109 --> 00:10:44,192
With this advertisement, AS3 promises AS2 that it will forward datagrams toward X.

82
00:10:44,642 --> 00:10:53,239
Next, AS2 gateway 2a advertises the path AS2, AS3, X to AS1 gateway 1c.

83
00:10:53,689 --> 00:10:58,169
AS2 adds its own number, AS2, at the front of the path.

84
00:10:58,619 --> 00:11:10,801
Now AS1 can send datagrams for X to AS2. The datagrams go from 1c, through 2a, 2b and 2c, then 3a and 3d, to X.

85
00:11:11,251 --> 00:11:15,603
So data flows in the opposite direction of the advertisements.

86
00:11:16,053 --> 00:11:25,589
An advertised BGP route has two parts: a prefix and attributes. Here is the route that AS2 sends to AS1.

87
00:11:26,039 --> 00:11:29,388
The prefix is the destination network, here X.

88
00:11:29,838 --> 00:11:38,329
The first important attribute is AS-PATH. It is the list of ASes that the advertisement has passed through.

89
00:11:38,779 --> 00:11:42,619
In this route, the AS-PATH is AS2, AS3.

90
00:11:43,069 --> 00:11:53,586
The second important attribute is NEXT-HOP. It gives the IP address of the advertising router's interface that is used for the eBGP session.

91
00:11:54,036 --> 00:12:00,969
Here it is the interface of router 2a. So NEXT-HOP names the router at the start of the next AS.

92
00:12:01,419 --> 00:12:13,963
BGP does policy-based routing, with two types of policy. A gateway that receives an advertisement uses its import policy to accept or decline the path.

93
00:12:14,413 --> 00:12:21,709
Example: never route through AS Y. If a path contains AS Y, the gateway does not use it.

94
00:12:22,159 --> 00:12:32,634
The policy of an AS also decides whether to advertise a learned path to other neighbour ASes. This is the export policy.

95
00:12:33,084 --> 00:12:46,183
Let us follow one advertisement. AS2 router 2c receives the path advertisement AS3, X from AS3 router 3a, by eBGP.

96
00:12:46,633 --> 00:12:53,246
AS2 has an import policy. Based on it, 2c accepts the path AS3, X.

97
00:12:53,696 --> 00:13:05,835
Then 2c propagates the path by iBGP to all gateway routers and iBGP-configured routers in AS2: here 2a, 2b and 2d.

98
00:13:06,285 --> 00:13:13,581
AS2 also has an export policy. It allows AS2 to advertise this path to AS1.

99
00:13:14,031 --> 00:13:23,439
So AS2 router 2a advertises the path AS2, AS3, X to AS1 router 1c, by eBGP.

100
00:13:23,889 --> 00:13:31,313
Now add a link between 1c and 3a. A gateway router can learn more than one path to a destination.

101
00:13:31,763 --> 00:13:38,227
First, AS1 gateway 1c learns the path AS2, AS3, X from 2a.

102
00:13:38,677 --> 00:13:43,434
Second, 1c learns the path AS3, X directly from 3a.

103
00:13:43,884 --> 00:13:48,279
Based on policy, 1c chooses the path AS3, X.

104
00:13:48,729 --> 00:13:54,254
Then 1c advertises this path inside AS1, by iBGP.

105
00:13:54,704 --> 00:13:59,504
Now 1a, 1b and 1d know that the path to X goes through 1c.

106
00:13:59,954 --> 00:14:08,018
Recall: 1a, 1b and 1d learn by iBGP from 1c that the path to X goes through 1c.

107
00:14:08,468 --> 00:14:13,289
The red numbers are the local interface numbers at 1a and 1d.

108
00:14:13,739 --> 00:14:22,230
Now look at router 1d. OSPF intra-domain routing tells 1d: to get to 1c, use interface 1.

109
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:26,093
So at 1d, to get to X, use interface 1.

110
00:14:26,543 --> 00:14:29,893
BGP gives the gateway for X: 1c.

111
00:14:30,343 --> 00:14:35,719
OSPF gives the interface toward that gateway: here, interface 1.

112
00:14:36,169 --> 00:14:44,894
Now router 1a. OSPF tells 1a: to get to 1c, use interface 2. Here the path goes through 1d.

113
00:14:45,344 --> 00:14:48,608
So at 1a, to get to X, use interface 2.

114
00:14:49,058 --> 00:14:54,050
In each table, the entry for X copies the entry for the gateway 1c.

115
00:14:54,500 --> 00:15:01,945
Recap. The Internet groups routers into autonomous systems, for scale and for administrative autonomy.

116
00:15:02,395 --> 00:15:07,153
Inside an AS, OSPF runs, with areas and a backbone.

117
00:15:07,603 --> 00:15:13,768
Between ASes, BGP runs, with eBGP and iBGP.

118
00:15:14,218 --> 00:15:23,989
A BGP route is a prefix plus attributes, such as AS-PATH and NEXT-HOP. Each AS adds its number to the AS-PATH.

119
00:15:24,439 --> 00:15:36,044
In the forwarding table, BGP gives the gateway, and OSPF gives the interface toward it. Video 20b continues with route selection and policy.
